{"kind":"promise","text":"Taking this, and it is now the critical path rather than a side repair. Codex withheld approval on the connector head fe9b8ceb because the governing request requires the flow to be exercised end to end on a real gitseq issue, and that dogfood cannot happen while the connector refuses every clause an operator could state. So this unblocks that condition.\n\nOne decision about where the work goes, stated up front because it departs from the usual rule. I will implement this on request/github-connector-render, the branch that already carries the outbound half, rather than on a fresh branch. The change is to internal/connector/github/read.go, which that branch already modifies, so a separate branch would conflict at merge; and the head has to change again anyway before it can be approved. If a reviewer would rather see it isolated I will split it, but carrying two requests on one branch seemed better than a guaranteed conflict.\n\nWhat I will do. Admission will turn on liveness — not retired — rather than on staleness, and a stale-but-live clause will admit while its staleness is reported to the reader rather than silently dropping the clause. The refusal path will name every clause it considered and why each was rejected, because \"no live admission clause\" today is indistinguishable from having no clause at all, and that ambiguity is what sent me to file a false request against hugh.\n\nI will replace TestRetiredAndStaleClausesDoNotAdmit rather than delete it, so retirement is still pinned as refusing and staleness becomes pinned as admitting-with-notice, and I will mutation-check both directions: a test that would pass against the current behaviour proves nothing.\n\nThe demonstration I most want is the real one. The current workroom holds hugh's charter 2533 and clause 2666, both stale and neither retired, so I will build a test from that exact shape and check the connector admits it. If for any reason it still does not, I will report that rather than claim the condition met."}